  "scenario": "A fleet telemetry system models abstract vehicles, concrete cars, their internal engines and wheels, and drivers who operate cars. The current diagram has two structural bugs: the inheritance arrow between Vehicle and Car points the wrong way, and Engine is modeled as loosely shared rather than exclusively owned by a Car.",
  "goal": "Inspect all existing relationships and fix the two incorrect ones so inheritance points from Car to Vehicle and Engine lifetime is strictly tied to Car.",
  "learningObjective": "Spot and correct reversed generalization arrows and distinguish strict composition from weak aggregation.",
  "hints": [
    "Generalization arrows always point from the subclass (Car) to the superclass (Vehicle).",
    "An Engine built inside a Car is owned exclusively by that Car and does not outlive it in this fleet model.",
    "Reverse the Vehicle-to-Car generalization so Car points to Vehicle, and change Car-to-Engine from Aggregation to Composition."
  ],
  "solutionExplanation": "Car inherits from Vehicle via Generalization (Car -> Vehicle). Car strictly owns its Engine and Wheels via Composition, while Driver operates Car via an undirected Association.",
